<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasan al-Utrush</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">844–917</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as al-Nasir al-Kabir, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Utrush</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasan al-Utrush, called al-Nasir al-Kabir, was a Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and Alid ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> on the Caspian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He converted many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilaks">Gilites</a> to Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and in 914 restored the Alavid state after its fall to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>. His legal works became the basis of the Nasiri school of Zaydi law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-al-utrush.jpg" alt="Hasan al-Utrush" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mirasir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeydiah_(zayydieh)_Naser_al_haqq_Tomb_tower_Shie_Mke_in_Iran.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
